/*
===========================================================
WHY WE WRITE TEST ANNOTATIONS AND ASSERTIONS SEPARATELY
===========================================================

Testing has TWO major responsibilities:

1️⃣ Control WHEN something runs  → (Annotations)
2️⃣ Verify WHAT happened         → (Assertions)

-----------------------------------------------------------
PART 1️⃣ : TEST ANNOTATIONS (Lifecycle Control)
-----------------------------------------------------------

Annotations control execution timing.

They do NOT verify logic.
They only control WHEN code runs.

-----------------------------------------------------------
@Test
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Marks a method as a test case.
• Without it, JUnit will NOT run the method.
• Used to define actual test scenarios.

When to use:
→ Every time you write a test case.

-----------------------------------------------------------
@Before
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Runs BEFORE each test method.
• Used for setup.
• Ensures test isolation.
• Creates fresh objects for each test.

When to use:
→ When you need a new instance before every test.
→ When resetting variables before each test.

Example usage:
Create new object.
Initialize mocks.
Prepare test data.

-----------------------------------------------------------
@After
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Runs AFTER each test method.
• Used for cleanup.
• Ensures no leftover state affects other tests.

When to use:
→ Clearing mocks.
→ Closing resources.
→ Resetting shared variables.

-----------------------------------------------------------
@BeforeClass
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Runs ONLY ONCE before ALL test methods.
• Must be static in JUnit 4.
• Used for heavy or global setup.

When to use:
→ Database connection setup.
→ Expensive initialization.
→ One-time configuration.

-----------------------------------------------------------
@AfterClass
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Runs ONLY ONCE after ALL tests complete.
• Used for global cleanup.

When to use:
→ Closing database.
→ Releasing global resources.

-----------------------------------------------------------
@Ignore
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Skips a test temporarily.
• Test will not execute.

When to use:
→ Feature not ready.
→ Test under development.
→ Temporary disabling.

-----------------------------------------------------------
PART 2️⃣ : ASSERTIONS (Verification)
-----------------------------------------------------------

Assertions verify behavior.

Without assertions:
→ Test will always PASS.
→ No real validation happens.

Assertions check expected vs actual result.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertEquals(expected, actual)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Checks if two values are equal.
• Most commonly used assertion.

Use when:
→ Verifying return values.
→ Comparing numbers or strings.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertNotEquals(expected, actual)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Checks values are NOT equal.

Use when:
→ Ensuring wrong value is not returned.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertTrue(condition)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Passes if condition is TRUE.

Use when:
→ Checking boolean conditions.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertFalse(condition)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Passes if condition is FALSE.

Use when:
→ Ensuring condition should not happen.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertNull(value)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Passes if value is NULL.

Use when:
→ Testing nullable returns.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertNotNull(value)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Passes if value is NOT NULL.

Use when:
→ Ensuring object was created.

-----------------------------------------------------------
assertThrows(Exception::class)
-----------------------------------------------------------
• Passes only if exception is thrown.

Use when:
→ Testing validation.
→ Testing error cases.

-----------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT DIFFERENCE
-----------------------------------------------------------

Annotations  → Control execution timing.
Assertions   → Validate results.

-----------------------------------------------------------
TEST EXECUTION FLOW (JUnit 4)
-----------------------------------------------------------

1️⃣ @BeforeClass   (Runs once)
2️⃣ @Before        (Runs before each test)
3️⃣ @Test          (Test logic runs)
4️⃣ Assertions     (Validation happens)
5️⃣ @After         (Cleanup after test)
6️⃣ Repeat steps 2–5 for each test
7️⃣ @AfterClass    (Runs once at end)

-----------------------------------------------------------
WHY WE DO NOT MIX EVERYTHING
-----------------------------------------------------------

• Keeps test clean and structured.
• Ensures isolation between tests.
• Makes debugging easier.
• Prevents shared-state bugs.
• Improves readability.
• Follows professional testing standards.

-----------------------------------------------------------
CORE PRINCIPLE
-----------------------------------------------------------

Annotations answer:
→ WHEN should this run?

Assertions answer:
→ IS the behavior correct?

Both are required for proper unit testing.
===========================================================
*/